  "id": "763257295",
  "subject": [
    "ANE"
  ],
  "title": "Ema\u0161ma\u0161",
  "provenance": "Pleiades",
  "placeTypeURIs": [
    "https://pleiades.stoa.org/vocabularies/place-types/temple-2"
  ],
  "details": "<p>The temple dates back to at least the third millennium B.C. and from the archaeological and textual records it had a long and detailed building history. Akkadian sources provide evidence for the following Akkadian and Assyrian kings rebuilding or repairing Ema\u0161ma\u0161: Man-i\u0161tu\u0161u, \u0160am\u0161\u012b-Adad I, A\u0161\u0161ur-uballi\u1e6d I, Shalmaneser I, Tukult\u012b-Ninurta I, A\u0161\u0161ur-r\u0113\u0161a-i\u0161i I, Tiglath-pileser I, Ashurnasirpal I, \u0160am\u0161\u012b-Adad IV, Ashurnasirpal II, Sargon II, Sennacherib, Esarhaddon, and Ashurbanipal. The Old Babylonian king Hammu-rapi also refers to this temple of I\u0161tar in his now-famous law code.</p>\r\n<p>The temple was excavated 1851-54, 1873-74, 1878-82, and 1929-32.</p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "The most important temple in the citadel of Nineveh was dedicated to the city's tutelary deity, the goddess I\u0161tar. The temple was located in the center of the citadel and went by the Sumerian ceremonial name Ema\u0161ma\u0161 (meaning uncertain).",
